<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>DataStudio.Marten</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>DataStudio.Marten</RootNamespace>
    <PackageId>DataStudio.Marten</PackageId>
    <Title>Data Studio for Marten</Title>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <Product>Data Studio</Product>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageTags>marten;martendb;postgresql;event-sourcing;document-database;admin;dashboard;ui;blazor;aspnetcore</PackageTags>
    <Description>An embeddable Blazor Server admin UI for MartenDB. Register it with services.AddDataStudio().AddMarten() and mount it with app.MapDataStudio() inside your own ASP.NET Core application; it runs against that application's own configured IDocumentStore. Browse document collections and JSON, inspect and steer event streams, projections and the async daemon, run guarded queries and manage schema. Every mutating operation is an individual capability flag and the studio refuses to start without an explicit authorization decision.</Description>
    <EnablePackageValidation>true</EnablePackageValidation>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Not trimmable, said out loud rather than left to the default. Blazor Server is a reflective
    framework by construction: a component's [Parameter] properties are set by name from the render
    tree, and the document viewer deserializes into types discovered from IDocumentType.DocumentType
    at run time. Marking this IsTrimmable would tell a trimmer it may cut into a graph nothing
    statically references. docs/setup.md (Hosting notes) says the same for a host.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsTrimmable>false</IsTrimmable>
    <IsAotCompatible>false</IsAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="../../README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />
    <None Include="../../icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Blazor Server, SignalR, authorization and the endpoint routing the studio mounts into all
         come from the shared framework, so the package pins no ASP.NET Core patch on its host. -->
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
    <!-- The JasperFx source generator Marten bundles is taken out of this compilation by the
         MartenWithoutTheJasperFxSourceGenerator target at the end of this file. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Marten" />
    <!-- The shared studio. The Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Internal.Assets reference - blazor.web.js for the
         host, D22 - lives in DataStudio.csproj (the shell) and reaches the host through this reference and DataStudio.Postgres's: two
         hops, measured by PS-0 on 2026-10-20 (200 for /_framework/blazor.web.js and under {Path}). -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../DataStudio.Postgres/DataStudio.Postgres.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DataStudio.Marten.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DataStudio.Marten.Integration.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The studio is compiled WITHOUT the JasperFx aggregate source generator that Marten bundles in its
    own analyzers/dotnet/cs folder.

    Why: the studio declares no aggregate and no projection, so the generator has nothing to generate
    here - at the 9.39.0 floor it emits no file for this assembly at all. But from JasperFx 2.75
    (Marten 9.40) it emits [assembly: JasperFx.Events.Aggregation.JasperFxSourceGeneratorApplied] into
    every assembly it processes, unconditionally, so a build at a newer Marten would put a JasperFx
    attribute on the shipped DataStudio.Marten.dll and into the public API baseline with it, while a build
    at the floor would not. Measured at 9.50.2, at 9.51.0 (JasperFx 2.91.0) and again at 9.54.1 (JasperFx 2.95.1): that marker is the only file it writes for this
    assembly, so nothing here relies on generated code. The marker is how the JasperFx runtime tells
    an assembly the generator processed from one it never saw (jasperfx#887); an assembly with no
    aggregate in it is not asked.

    Why a target and not ExcludeAssets="analyzers" on the Marten reference: that was tried first and
    measured to do nothing (SDK 10.0.402, 2026-10-15, in this project and in a minimal one without
    central package management). NuGet records the exclusion - the include flags in
    project.assets.json drop Analyzers - but the SDK's ResolvePackageAssets adds the analyzers of every
    package in the lock file without consulting them, and the generator still ran. ExcludeAssets also
    changes nothing in the nuspec: the dependency is exclude="Build,Analyzers" either way, byte for byte.

    This file is not packed, so a consumer sees none of it: a host's own Marten reference brings the
    generator to the host's own projections as before. Only this compilation loses it - the sample and
    both test projects keep it, which they need (the 9.22.4 to 9.23.0 "no source-generated dispatcher"
    wall in AGENTS.md). JasperFxSourceGeneratorTests pins both halves.
    BeforeTargets="CoreCompile", like JasperFx.Events' own dedupe target, so design-time builds see the
    same analyzer list as a real one.
  -->
  <Target Name="MartenWithoutTheJasperFxSourceGenerator" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_MartenJasperFxGenerator Include="@(Analyzer)"
                                      Condition="'%(Filename)%(Extension)' == 'JasperFx.Events.SourceGenerator.dll'" />
      <Analyzer Remove="@(_MartenJasperFxGenerator)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    The package is packed at the Marten floor and nowhere else. A nupkg packed against 9.54.1 would
    say `Marten >= 9.54.1` and silently raise the floor of every host that takes it; the floor is a
    decision recorded in AGENTS.md, not a side effect of which leg of CI ran Pack. The build's Pack
    target refuses first and says why; this is the same refusal for a bare `dotnet pack`.

    Two checks, because the property is not what NuGet packs from. Pack writes the dependency range
    from project.assets.json - whatever the last restore wrote - so `dotnet test ...
    -p:DataStudioMartenVersion=9.54.1` followed by `dotnet pack` with no-restore or no-build would
    pass a property-only check at the floor and still write `Marten >= 9.54.1`. So the guard also
    reads $(ProjectAssetsFile) and requires the library key "Marten/<floor>" in it, quotes included,
    so that a package whose name merely ends in Marten cannot satisfy it; a graph holds one version of
    a package, so the key's presence is the resolved version. The quotes are spelled \x22 in the
    regular expression so that no literal quote has to survive MSBuild's argument parsing. It fails
    closed: no assets file at pack time is a refusal, not a pass. (In an ordinary pack NuGet's own
    GetProjectReferencesFromAssetsFileTask refuses a missing file first; this branch is the guard not
    depending on that, and was exercised by running the target directly.) Measured 2026-10-15: after a
    restore at 9.50.2, pack with no-restore and pack with no-build both fail here and write no nupkg,
    and a pack at the floor writes the same nuspec byte for byte.
  -->
  <Target Name="MartenPackOnlyAtTheMartenFloor" BeforeTargets="GenerateNuspec">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_MartenAssetsFileExists>false</_MartenAssetsFileExists>
      <_MartenAssetsFileExists Condition="'$(ProjectAssetsFile)' != '' and Exists('$(ProjectAssetsFile)')">true</_MartenAssetsFileExists>
      <_MartenFloorLibraryKey>"Marten/$(DataStudioMartenFloor)"</_MartenFloorLibraryKey>
      <_MartenFloorLibraryPattern>\x22Marten/$(DataStudioMartenFloor.Replace('.', '\.'))\x22</_MartenFloorLibraryPattern>
      <_MartenAssets Condition="'$(_MartenAssetsFileExists)' == 'true'">$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('$(ProjectAssetsFile)'))</_MartenAssets>
      <_MartenAssetsAtFloor>false</_MartenAssetsAtFloor>
      <_MartenAssetsAtFloor Condition="'$(_MartenAssetsFileExists)' == 'true' and $([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch($(_MartenAssets), $(_MartenFloorLibraryPattern)))">true</_MartenAssetsAtFloor>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <Error Condition="'$(DataStudioMartenVersion)' != '$(DataStudioMartenFloor)'"
           Text="DataStudio.Marten is packed only at the Marten floor ($(DataStudioMartenFloor), Directory.Packages.props), and this pack asks for Marten $(DataStudioMartenVersion). A package packed here would depend on Marten &gt;= $(DataStudioMartenVersion) and raise every host's floor. Pack without DataStudioMartenVersion, or raise DataStudioMartenFloor deliberately and record why in AGENTS.md, Package budget changes." />
    <Error Condition="'$(_MartenAssetsFileExists)' != 'true'"
           Text="DataStudio.Marten is packed only at the Marten floor ($(DataStudioMartenFloor)), and there is no restore output to prove it ('$(ProjectAssetsFile)' does not exist). NuGet writes the package's dependency range from that file, so the guard refuses rather than guess. Pack with a restore (without --no-restore) at the floor." />
    <Error Condition="'$(_MartenAssetsFileExists)' == 'true' and '$(_MartenAssetsAtFloor)' != 'true'"
           Text="DataStudio.Marten is packed only at the Marten floor ($(DataStudioMartenFloor)), but the last restore ('$(ProjectAssetsFile)') resolved another Marten - it has no $(_MartenFloorLibraryKey) library. NuGet writes the package's dependency range from that file, so this pack would raise every host's floor. Restore at the floor first: pack without --no-restore and without DataStudioMartenVersion." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    DataStudio.Marten depends on exactly its own DataStudio.Postgres: `DataStudio.Postgres [0.5.0]` in the nuspec, not
    NuGet's default `>= 0.5.0`. The two assemblies are one program split in two files - DataStudio.Postgres
    grants DataStudio.Marten its internals - so DataStudio.Marten 0.5.0 beside DataStudio.Postgres 0.5.1 is a
    MissingMethodException on the first internal signature that moved. Measured by PS-0 (2026-10-20, SDK
    10.0.402): with this target the nuspec says version="[1.2.3]", without it version="1.2.3".

    How: GenerateNuspec hands PackTask @(_ProjectReferencesWithVersions), which _GetProjectReferenceVersions
    fills with each referenced project's $(PackageVersion) as ProjectVersion metadata, and PackTask reads
    that metadata as a version RANGE. Bracketing it after that target is the whole change. Both names are
    NuGet-private, so the build's Pack target also asserts the nuspec text: a NuGet that renames them fails
    the build instead of shipping `>=`. The Error is the second half of the same defence: a pack that finds
    no DataStudio.Postgres project reference to pin refuses rather than shipping a package with no pin at all.

    A host that ends up with another DataStudio.Postgres anyway - a central PackageVersion under transitive
    pinning drops it from the graph with no NuGet diagnostic at all (PS-0) - is stopped at startup by
    Internal/PostgresVersionGuard.cs.
  -->
  <Target Name="DataStudioMartenPinsPostgresExactly" AfterTargets="_GetProjectReferenceVersions">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_ProjectReferencesWithVersions Condition="'%(Filename)' == 'DataStudio.Postgres'">
        <ProjectVersion>[%(ProjectVersion)]</ProjectVersion>
      </_ProjectReferencesWithVersions>
    </ItemGroup>
    <Error Condition="'@(_ProjectReferencesWithVersions->WithMetadataValue('Filename', 'DataStudio.Postgres'))' == ''"
           Text="DataStudio.Marten must depend on exactly its own DataStudio.Postgres build, and pack found no DataStudio.Postgres project reference to pin." />
  </Target>

</Project>
